# Headhunter Agent (Agent 5)

## Identity

* **Type:** In-process Python daemon (same pattern as Aligner).
* **Role:** GitLab MR lifecycle automation. Polls `/todos` API, classifies MRs, creates events for Brain.
* **Behavior:** Autonomous, FIFO. Processes oldest todos first. Does NOT route to agents directly -- creates events for Brain.

## Architecture

* **File:** `src/agents/headhunter.py`
* **Pattern:** Poll -> Classify -> Create Event -> Brain handles routing
* **LLM:** gemini-4-argon via `_get_adapter()` (env: `LLM_MODEL_HEADHUNTER`, `LLM_TEMPERATURE_HEADHUNTER`, `LLM_MAX_TOKENS_HEADHUNTER`). System instruction loaded from `headhunter_skills/gitlab-mr-triage.md` via `_load_gitlab_si()` (fallback: `_EMERGENCY_SI`).
* **Flow Gate:** Global WIP cap (`MAX_ACTIVE_EVENTS`) controls event creation. Headhunter's `check_flow_gate()` queries the global WIP count via injected `Brain.count_global_wip(include_new=True)` (or local fallback), which subtracts `_waiting_for_user` events to free capacity for incoming work. Backs off when system is at capacity.
* **Circuit Breaker:** 3 consecutive failures -> self-disable. Brain continues.
* **Startup Delay:** 180s default (env: `HEADHUNTER_STARTUP_DELAY`). Waits for sidecars.

## LLM Analysis

All MRs go through gemini-4-argon analysis with the system instruction from `headhunter_skills/gitlab-mr-triage.md` (loaded via `_load_gitlab_si()`, externalized skill file pattern). The SI defines output format (YAML frontmatter matching `create_plan` schema), domain classification, agent assignment, risk assessment, and downstream Brain awareness (merge semantics, pipeline verification rules).

| Path | Trigger | How |
|---|---|---|
| **LLM Analysis** | All MRs | gemini-4-argon reads full MR context (description, pipeline, changed files, comments) and generates a YAML plan. No input truncation — full description flows to gemini-4-argon (1M+ context). Bot Instructions in the description are included as prompt context. Output controlled by `LLM_MAX_TOKENS_HEADHUNTER`. |
| **Emergency Fallback** | LLM unavailable | Inline `_emergency_plan()` produces COMPLICATED/developer plan. Safety net only. |

## Maintainer Resolution

Chain: `HEADHUNTER_MAINTAINERS` (static CSV) -> GitLab user email lookup -> MR metadata fallback.

* Static list is read at runtime (not import time) via `_get_static_maintainer_emails()`.
* `HEADHUNTER_MAINTAINER_SOURCE`: `static` (default) or `smartsheet` (future).
* Resolved maintainer stored in `evidence.gitlab_context.maintainer` at event creation.

## Security: @Mention Instruction Injection

* MR comments tagging the configured bot account (`GITLAB_BOT_USERNAME` env) are passed as plan context.
* Only authors in the allowed list can inject instructions (default-closed).
* Allowed authors = email local parts from `HEADHUNTER_MAINTAINERS` + explicit `HEADHUNTER_ALLOWED_AUTHORS` CSV.
* If both are empty, all @mentions are blocked (`_nobody_` sentinel).

## Feedback Loop (Signal + Poll Hybrid)

* Brain signals `_headhunter_close_signal.set()` in `_close_and_broadcast()`.
* Headhunter feedback loop wakes on signal or every `poll_interval` (safety net).
* Posts MR comment + marks todo as done on event close.
* `directly_addressed` and `mentioned` bypass recently-processed dedup (explicit re-request).

## Dedup Layers

| Layer | Platform | What it catches | Crash-safe? |
|---|---|---|---|
| `get_active_keys()` (Blackboard active events) | GitLab `(project, mr)`, GitHub `(owner, repo, number)` | Second event for an MR/PR/Issue that already has an active/deferred headhunter event | Yes |
| Flow gate (`MAX_ACTIVE_EVENTS`) | both | Concurrent events beyond the global WIP cap | Yes |
| GitLab todo `mark_as_done` | GitLab | Re-processing the same todo after the event closes | Yes |
| GitHub label truth table (`darwin-active` > `darwin-review` > `darwin-queued` > `darwin-done`) + `darwin:github:pr_sha` | GitHub PR | Re-trigger only on a new head SHA after `darwin-done` | Yes |
| `darwin:github:issue:{o}:{r}:{n}` (7d) | GitHub Issue | Re-processing an Issue after a label API failure | Yes |
| `darwin:github:pr_feedback_cursor` + per-item `darwin:github:pr_feedback:{o}:{r}:{pr}:{kind}:{id}` (SET NX, 7d) | GitHub review feedback | At-least-once handling of each review / inline / issue comment, deduped by seen ID | Yes |

## GitHub Review-Response Trigger (flag: `HEADHUNTER_GITHUB_REVIEW_RESPONSE_ENABLED`, default off)

* **Trigger A (`review_feedback`):** trusted `CHANGES_REQUESTED` / `COMMENTED` (non-empty body) reviews, inline `/pulls/{n}/comments`, and issue comments on open, non-draft PRs authored by `{GITHUB_APP_SLUG}[bot]`.
* **Trigger B (`mention`):** a trusted comment/review containing `@darwin-project-ai` on an open, non-draft, non-fork PR in an installed repo. Drafts and fork PRs (`head.repo.fork`, or head/base repo `full_name` differ) are skipped so a fork head can never reach branch checkout/execution.
* **Trust gate (fail-closed):** `author_association` in `HEADHUNTER_GITHUB_REVIEW_TRUSTED_ASSOCIATIONS` (default `OWNER,MEMBER,COLLABORATOR`) AND not `user.type == "Bot"` AND not `*[bot]` AND not in `HEADHUNTER_GITHUB_REVIEW_IGNORE_LOGINS` (default `openshift-ci-robot,openshift-merge-robot`), plus an in-memory TTL-cached (10 min) `/repos/{o}/{r}/collaborators/{login}/permission` check requiring `write`/`maintain`/`admin` on trigger items. Darwin's own login is always ignored (loop guard). The association/bot filter also filters `fetch_context().recent_comments`.
* **Bootstrap & watermark:** a persistent Redis rollout watermark (`darwin:github:review_response_watermark`, `SET NX` on first enabled poll) separates pre-rollout history from post-rollout PRs. First sight of a PR marks only items older than the watermark as seen (no backfill on rollout), while post-watermark items on brand-new PRs are delivered normally through debounce + dedup. There is NO id floor afterwards: a pending (drafted) review's comments get their id at draft time, so a late-submitted one can be lower than the cursor. Delivery is at-least-once with seen-ID dedup: per-item SET NX seen keys (7d TTL) + a 6-day age cutoff; items are marked seen only after the event exists or the append to the owner event succeeded, so a crash in between can re-deliver but never drop. The cursor `ts` only drives `since` on issue comments and the `updated_at` rescan gate for non-Darwin PRs.
* **Bounded batches:** at most 20 items / ~`HEADHUNTER_COMMENT_LIMIT`x10 chars per cycle, oldest first. Only delivered items are marked seen and the cursor `ts` does not advance while items remain, so the rest are appended to the active event next cycle (never dropped).
* **Debounce / rounds:** event only when the newest item is >= `HEADHUNTER_GITHUB_REVIEW_DEBOUNCE_SEC` old; after `HEADHUNTER_GITHUB_REVIEW_MAX_ROUNDS` rounds one PR comment + one `review_round_cap` notification event (NX key rolled back on emission failure), then silence. Reset a PR: `HDEL darwin:github:pr_feedback_rounds {o}:{r}:{n}` and `DEL darwin:github:pr_feedback_cap:{o}:{r}:{n}`.
* **Active event:** if any active OR `waiting_approval` event references the PR, feedback is appended to it as an `actor=headhunter/action=evidence` turn (sanitized `<review_feedback>` fence, labelled untrusted) instead of creating a second event. `source=headhunter` events always win ties over non-headhunter events, and `casual`-domain Slack/chat threads are excluded from hijacking PR feedback. If the owner is gone/`CLOSED` or the append fails, items stay unseen and the new-event path runs. Brain treats that turn like user input for `deferred` (early wake) and `active` + `waiting_for_user` (bypass) events, but it does NOT clear the `WAITING_APPROVAL` human gate (only a real `user` turn does), and it is consumed by the normal DELIVERED->PROCESSED path (no re-wake after Brain answers).
* **No labels:** review-response events never apply `darwin-*` labels or write `pr_sha` (see `_NO_LABEL_ACTIONS`).
* **Plan shape:** `headhunter_skills/github-pr-review-response.md` (developer fix -> qe verify -> developer reply). Merge safety (disable auto-merge before push, no Prow `/lgtm`/`/approve`) lives in that skill and `brain_skills/dispatch/mr-lifecycle.md`.
* **Phase D** in `_github_poll_and_process` (after issues), only with the flow gate open. Any Phase D error is non-fatal (logged; never feeds the GitHub circuit breaker): one PR's 429/5xx keeps healthy PRs' items, and only a 2+ PR poll where every PR failed 429/5xx raises. API budget: <=3 GETs per polled PR per cycle when each endpoint has <=100 items; pagination is bounded to 5 pages per endpoint (<=15 GETs worst case); non-Darwin PRs are only rescanned when `updated_at` moved past the cursor.

## Brain Tool: `refresh_gitlab_context`

The Headhunter equivalent of `re_trigger_aligner`. Closes the observe-and-react loop.

* **Mechanism:** Brain calls `refresh_gitlab_context` -> handler calls `headhunter.refresh_mr_state(event_id)` -> Headhunter fetches current MR + pipeline state from GitLab API -> updates `evidence.gitlab_context` + reclassifies `evidence.severity` -> returns snapshot to Brain conversation.
* **Two patterns:**
  * **Pre-dispatch triage:** Brain refreshes before `select_agent` to give agents precise instructions based on current state.
  * **Post-defer verification:** After deferring for a running pipeline, Brain refreshes to check outcome.
* **Error handling:** GitLab 5xx/429/404 return error dicts; evidence is NOT mutated on error.
* **Headhunter disabled:** Handler returns graceful fallback message; Brain can use `select_agent` to check MR state manually.
* **Registration:** `brain.agents["_headhunter"] = headhunter` in `main.py` (post-construction, follows `_aligner` pattern).

## Severity Classification

`_classify_severity(action_name, pipeline_status)` maps MR context to event severity at creation time and on refresh:

| Action | Pipeline | Severity |
|---|---|---|
| `build_failed` | any | `warning` |
| `unmergeable` | any | `warning` |
| any | `failed` | `warning` |
| `review_requested` | success/unknown | `info` |
| `approval_required` | success/unknown | `info` |
| `assigned` | success/unknown | `info` |

Brain can override via `classify_event(severity=...)` -> stored in `brain_severity` (separate from source classification).

## Key Invariants

* Sidecar skills NEVER include GitLab usernames in Slack recommendations.
* Brain ALWAYS uses `evidence.gitlab_context.maintainer.emails` for notifications.
* `evidence.gitlab_context.mr_description` contains the original MR description (Bot Instructions visible to Brain).
* `GITLAB_BOT_USERNAME` env var controls which account name to match in @mention scanning.

## Prompt Injection Defense

* XML fence sanitization via `_sanitize_xml_fence()` in `headhunter.py`. Strips closing tags for all prompt XML fences (`</description>`, `</job_log>`, `</comments>`, `</mention_request>`, `</issue_body>`, `</review_feedback>`) from user-controlled content before embedding.
* `mention_author` sanitized (quote stripping) before XML attribute insertion.
* `_DESC_SAFETY_CAP` (env: `HEADHUNTER_DESCRIPTION_CAP`, default 100K) prevents degenerate descriptions from consuming excessive tokens.

